#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078508 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4f1e3e2-5c46-5743-beb7-02c84848a023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232100}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232100} :answer-field/id #uuid "300e34bd-5285-5c13-a537-aa100dbd6357" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9f40d5a-5c40-5cea-a71f-e6bd6f18a1ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-131287" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 2}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as we approach $x=2$ from the left, the $y$-value approaches $0.$ So the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $x=2$ from the right, the $y$-value again approaches $0.$ So the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $0,$ we conclude that\n\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068910 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050454}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50404f8c-92b8-56c0-8102-a42994addb37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232102}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232102} :answer-field/id #uuid "56253d20-39c9-5075-b061-d08dedd165db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232101} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2439d07f-6b70-574b-97c2-329546f983ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232104}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232104} :answer-field/id #uuid "daa9af27-3087-5114-9f1a-9b2d236061b0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e7fd0c3c-4ab0-5efb-afc4-30ed5931b664" :question/math-academy-id "q-131292" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the function $g(x)$ approaches $2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the function $g(x)$ approaches $0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124579 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050259}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a36fb63d-3c31-5cbb-b9cf-042c3f9071ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232106} :answer-field/id #uuid "36f0d724-ded6-59d7-b4e1-a20b672e13a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232105} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd4e4ad0-a97b-5199-bd79-e6155be571ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232108}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232108} :answer-field/id #uuid "de40da47-2a6b-5a6a-b84b-119162ea8ee0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "170a31e3-1d72-5b3b-8cb5-bd7b9571e6a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-131293" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x).$ If a limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function $g(x)$ moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=4.$\n\nConsequently, $g(x)$ approaches the value $4$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=4.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty $ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124613 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050653}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b0451a7-d3ae-5539-982b-cdba479dfc2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232110}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232110} :answer-field/id #uuid "671d7e52-4565-556f-bca1-eca86381b51e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "beec162e-13c1-5c61-b8bd-bacbffaa3355" :question/math-academy-id "q-131295" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2}).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the sum and constant multiple rules, we can compute the required limit as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2})=2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)-3{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)+{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe above equation assumes that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)$ both exist.\n\nIn part 5, we found that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2,$ and from part 3, we found ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(2f(x)-3g(x)+{x}^{2}) & =2{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)-3{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}g(x)+{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 0+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-0+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115326 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050304}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2719497b-b860-53c4-a27c-4d8fde1ddcf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232112} :answer-field/id #uuid "add9b81c-1681-55bc-bb7c-56380c8e2ad4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eb198c75-6fba-5a7c-800a-b8f91fa89f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-131296" :question/problem "Given that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}Cf(x)=6,$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C?$\n\n$C=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2.$\n\nWe factor the constant out of the limit and evaluate the remaining limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}Cf(x) & =6 \\\\ C\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x) & =6 \\\\ C\\cdot 2 & =6 \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e30ffac-ef48-5ebc-9d4a-1d35a21e83ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232114}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232114} :answer-field/id #uuid "db600ccf-8c9e-5818-8986-4a5183834cdc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e3e02ef1-99bc-5d3e-bbb4-b92b55e0a600" :question/math-academy-id "q-131299" :question/problem "What are the points of discontinuity of $f(x)?$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The point of discontinuity of $f(x)$ is $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of this point without taking our pencil off of the page."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078508 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2cf078-b1db-56e2-800b-99fa042e51e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232116}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232116} :answer-field/id #uuid "f57b87a7-a089-51c6-8397-5fc3abb1606d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "62c0d71c-1e92-567f-8bba-65838bb0e669" :question/math-academy-id "q-131320" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "455c4b6e-110d-5034-8206-9e51a2efd0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232118}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232118} :answer-field/id #uuid "88cd1965-5860-5342-95c3-f8140755ad3a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232117} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e8bc2d6-2470-56a9-b363-29e61d027a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232120} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bbe0a11-9e06-58e6-880f-d1506b7f7109" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e474938b-ccf5-57f3-a574-52ab0dbbc7f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-131334" :question/problem "**In ascending order**, what are the points of discontinuity of $g(x)?$\n\n$g(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The points of discontinuity of $g(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=2.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these points without taking our pencil off of the page."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068701 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f4a6397-598d-5801-bf97-52d4f247282c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232122}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232122} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cde4b1a-26bd-50b1-aca6-549f5ea87558" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7cb9c6cb-9439-5b39-b7d2-249b0c0fa12d" :question/math-academy-id "q-133024" :question/problem "Find the value of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$f(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $4$ for $x$ in the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4\\sqrt{x}-2 \\\\ f(4) & =4\\sqrt{4}-2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2-2 \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059632 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d57ec02-ba69-5054-b3a1-f1c6aa956601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232124} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0eb2556-c891-53c6-831d-643167ac3324" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eeb6fffe-696e-54b9-8d97-330aef5d8411" :question/math-academy-id "q-133025" :question/problem "$h(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x>2,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=5-x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =5-4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129109 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049472}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d48e91d-4180-54b8-b737-ad8ec744c37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1/3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232126} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d939a8-5e7b-5142-afaf-b344aeb03f4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2fbc0c7f-96a2-5111-9e11-18b90dce4231" :question/math-academy-id "q-133026" :question/problem "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\nThe root is at $x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 3x-1 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124564 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed913ff0-fada-588a-bd62-1043f852e2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232128} :answer-field/id #uuid "35de9dd3-7057-5dda-9c54-89267cc1e414" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5d174bb2-878f-5844-bb8e-ec48e34806d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-133027" :question/problem "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-1,6],$ find the global minimum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n${h}_{min}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n* A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $h(x)=-2.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc25d9452e02cfd80ae586368897ef3d917ab9bc5c8e7ea1b74c154f1d4ebb19.png)\n\nTherefore, the global minimum is ${h}_{min}=-2.$ The function reaches this value when $x=1,$ so the global minimum point is $(1,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054613 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050782}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "724f318f-79a0-5d54-812b-d3ea8779379f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+4\\sqrt{x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232130} :answer-field/id #uuid "74758a0c-deb9-5f7e-90c8-83627fb8fdb8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90fd9136-df1b-53a0-8c59-56447a83e5ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-133028" :question/problem "Write the expression for the function $(f+g)(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$(f+g)(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(4\\sqrt{x}-2)+(3x-1) \\\\  & =3x+4\\sqrt{x}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054612 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050782}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "373c0caf-d013-58da-b571-a799851e8a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232132} :answer-field/id #uuid "17aecf15-7f6e-5216-bd4f-0ac178a0ce0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9cd49990-66ce-5535-aa4a-c7bbf124f0fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-133029" :question/problem "Find the value of $(f/h)(4).$\n\n$(f/h)(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/h)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $h(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/h)(4),$ we need to divide $f(4)$ by $h(4).$\n\nEarlier, we found the following values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =6 \\\\ h(4) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n(f/h)(4)=\\frac{f(4)}{h(4)}=\\frac{6}{1}=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129298 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050633}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bb9d4cd-f4d6-5d21-85c5-40db5641741a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232134}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232134} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc92d4a-71bf-5a6d-8fbe-85812046fc88" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "40f66893-d09c-539f-9da2-b5f8f880a871" :question/math-academy-id "q-133030" :question/problem "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$ \n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=1.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=1$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{1}-2 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233403 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179650 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "875c4734-04eb-5672-b2cf-1ee563bb8a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232136}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232136} :answer-field/id #uuid "d62759ec-05a8-5b03-9b08-48ab9d79036c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232135} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9011212-47f3-51b3-a4bc-072bffc80dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232138} :answer-field/id #uuid "d440d247-294b-57b2-933f-a416b4a9a004" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a467c021-98c1-5ee3-85de-370261e5fed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-133031" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x)$ for $x\\in [-1,1].$ What is its range over the given domain?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,g(x)\\,\\le $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function $g(x)=3x-1.$\n\n![](images/81/819903d614018e7945117ddd14b26cf669777c1d4f3742e095b1cebc212bf879.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-4.$ This occurs at the point $(-1,-4).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $2.$ This occurs at the point $(1,2).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-4$ and $2,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-4\\le y\\le 2,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-4\\le g(x)\\le 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050867}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c6e14b9-8dd9-5089-b765-4c15029049dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1/3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232140} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f995e7-f66e-5aab-88fc-e3a41b5d9ee3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eda81c69-e6b5-560b-916a-aea89aa3fd16" :question/math-academy-id "q-133032" :question/problem "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{3}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=3x-1$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x-1)+k \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{3}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{3}+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-\\frac{1}{3}+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}+k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068701 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1941800-ba36-5bfe-b5d0-f7b721ef66d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232142}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232142} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f3e6d6-bba6-5472-b7fb-00e2b9679af5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "31be6db1-da1a-53e1-ac7b-784594c536e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-133092" :question/problem "Find the value of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$f(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $4$ for $x$ in the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ f(4) & =3\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059632 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7fc6f5-9351-59ed-a0a8-6bbf3abe5e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232144} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2cf1cbf-8101-507a-9bd5-a4c440a4f1e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d12ecc54-779c-5e7e-b525-08121dfefd9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-133093" :question/problem "$h(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x>3,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=2x-5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =2(4)-5 \\\\  & =8-5 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129109 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049472}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e1059ff-2be0-5e45-b698-a9e7e07cb791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232146}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232146} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed9b8b9c-47be-51ac-bef2-cbbd7c257ddb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c34d8b7-e6af-57e7-a793-a1fe2bec9f88" :question/math-academy-id "q-133094" :question/problem "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\nThe root is at $x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 3x-2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x}{3} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124564 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c1df7c-d077-56ba-b9af-fbdca4377967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232148}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232148} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f42ece6-6692-5e5e-9946-2c9713ba7127" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8a8a3df3-95ec-568e-81a7-61672451d538" :question/math-academy-id "q-133095" :question/problem "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5],$ find the global minimum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n${h}_{min}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n* A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $h(x)=-4.$\n\n![](images/f7/f76f7203f1ba17f8148bbc1140420034a631f46e6995a3bda5453ec3fd9a6f55.png)\n\nTherefore, the global minimum is ${h}_{min}=-4.$ The function reaches this value when $x=1,$ so the global minimum point is $(1,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054613 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050782}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4756d6eb-052c-5896-a649-68c2e326ad7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+3\\sqrt{x+1}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232150}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232150} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffcea054-4dce-5181-b0eb-f346ce68885c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58c2b766-d884-5912-a599-48299b5395cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-133096" :question/problem "Write the expression for the function $(f+g)(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$(f+g)(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(3\\sqrt{x+1})+(3x-2) \\\\  & =3x+3\\sqrt{x+1}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054612 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050782}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342df5f2-e5aa-5de6-b670-3cb8f7227af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232152} :answer-field/id #uuid "0945b794-4e13-5e27-bf71-ff2f58be3150" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e81c74c-d8bf-5e75-98f6-1743f2977bfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-133097" :question/problem "Find the value of $(f/h)(4).$\n\n$(f/h)(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/h)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $h(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/h)(4),$ we need to divide $f(4)$ by $h(4).$\n\nEarlier, we found the following values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =3\\sqrt{5} \\\\ h(4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n(f/h)(4)=\\frac{f(4)}{h(4)}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{3}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129298 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050633}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96891483-b5f8-5435-a0c6-35c36270f687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232154}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232154} :answer-field/id #uuid "700dbcaa-06d0-50bf-aac3-249ce46e99ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1fbf806a-83ee-585a-87af-d5d9932264ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-133098" :question/problem "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$\n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=3.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=3$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(3) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179650 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d63696a-fc10-5c1f-bbab-49714264701c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232156}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232156} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee1dbd56-937b-5fba-bbae-4743591e0622" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232155} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3035fea2-ffa2-5c31-8b91-b8ac18c32a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232158} :answer-field/id #uuid "8649c76b-1dce-5a41-a359-5fa430191597" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3d82cc85-9159-5053-88d0-4969cb3405ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-133099" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x)$ for $x\\in [0,2].$ What is its range over the given domain?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,g(x)\\,\\le $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function $g(x)=3x-2.$\n\n![](images/47/47eb01a66773e5cf2b223cc3f8f4ff02caa424170a8e090ec0b1e45ed6d69741.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [0,2].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-2.$ This occurs at the point $(0,-2).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $4.$ This occurs at the point $(2,4).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-2$ and $4,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-2\\le y\\le 4,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-2\\le g(x)\\le 4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050867}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "446644fe-7fdc-5219-8de9-9860965a239f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232160} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e471da-72a6-5e13-a229-73c04ea604b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f42be186-ad03-5356-9e64-f3236e21d772" :question/math-academy-id "q-133100" :question/problem "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{3}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=3x-2$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{3}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(3x-2)+k \\\\  & =x-\\frac{2}{3}+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-\\frac{2}{3}+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-\\frac{2}{3}+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}+k & =0 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115186 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049442}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99255c4c-5558-5417-ba91-7c0183769869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232162} :answer-field/id #uuid "be028048-638b-5cc3-bf05-4c507685304c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6a70ed2f-24be-520f-bc29-361c3cb620d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-133321" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115187 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049442}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd93faec-2150-5f36-b61a-38ef54bfd10d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232164}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232164} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b1cdb16-5d8d-5a62-a087-0ed8f98ddd07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9bc1c9e9-c3fa-5468-93cc-68237790e0b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-133322" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right).$\n\n![](images/72/72a0459ee5db59562a19961d0304785fb8d46db8be24ad7839d4b8690b0dc835.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases to $-\\infty $, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-6$ and $y=6$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=DNE.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100833 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048156}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b5e4c82-03ad-5613-8969-930796d66c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232166} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc163ba8-f876-5afb-aa02-7c24d1ad0781" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232165}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aba4b4b3-386a-57af-b8b5-eafdd6f93386" :question/math-academy-id "q-133323" :question/problem "What is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [3\\pi ,3\\pi +h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n$\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right).$ Notice that we are given the length $h$ of the interval. So, the average rate of change of $f$ over this interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwith $a=3\\pi .$ Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3\\pi +h)-f(3\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{1}{3}(3\\pi +h))-6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})-6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})-6(0)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078508 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1cda491-9182-55c7-a31b-42567697cd68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232168}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232168} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd76a93d-b1dd-5136-b14d-debb6025c217" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7442d10c-b6cd-50b8-a990-9a01b0bdd6bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-133324" :question/problem "![](images/11/1186087a359b3065400101c7f436c3e885bf937477bdd9f73fbdaab6771aa48a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3}).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-2,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124669 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050916}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25133b7b-5236-5b36-91ef-d3e47673169f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232170} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d54f1e9-8080-50cc-99bd-ab2a6b4c9b21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3cbafae4-2598-54df-ac1f-4d1b38dbf7e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133325" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right).$\n\n![](images/42/42869c56604cbec85b5e4915f77b42e72444b7d9d117e140d9eba56b58c0f131.png)\n\nFrom the graph, as $x$ approaches $3\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)$ are positive and increase without bound. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}={lim}_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119937 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d075393-f0b1-5050-9701-89f8f9f39aed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1/4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232172} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1f622c4-f116-5287-bc1f-3b3e1b182cd8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "da35ebe3-4d6d-54a5-a5ee-e301bd3e21bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-133326" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{2-2}{(2)^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator and the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & =\\frac{x-2}{(x-2)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{1}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100824 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049226}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab7b9a9-97dc-5def-9fe8-f610c73625f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232174} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b642f1-0b89-5fae-ae37-34937e75b5da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a350247e-7ecd-5d52-833a-6128b4df7d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-133327" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the product rule to the original limit, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}[\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot f(x)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054605 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050987}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5602b6a-97b1-5715-b8f8-0fc2607acd3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232176} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3507c60-ea9d-51a9-bcd9-b5650979cebd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aec238f6-9664-599e-9ce5-a870d0a029f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-133328" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\sqrt{3}f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n* applying the constant multiple rule in the numerator, and \n\n* the root rule, followed by the sum rule in the denominator, \n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(\\sqrt{3}f(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3)^{2}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)}{{({lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi }(2x+3))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (3\\sqrt{3})}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{(2\\pi +3)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092244 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050807}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5acf83a-caaa-5169-be3f-4396f340e3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232178} :answer-field/id #uuid "24ba2edc-60a0-5111-8e1f-e2c5236c6e13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a50a260c-43fe-5579-b541-bb31003721ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-133329" :question/problem "Find the third partial sum ${s}_{3}$ of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{f(6n\\pi +\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{n}}.$\n\n${s}_{3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by calculating the first three terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 1+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{1}}=\\frac{f(7\\pi )}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}=3 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 2+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}=\\frac{f(13\\pi )}{3}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{13\\pi }{3}\\right)}{3}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot 3}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{f(6\\pi \\cdot 3+\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{3}}=\\frac{f(19\\pi )}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{19\\pi }{3}\\right)}{3\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{6\\sqrt{3}}{2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+{a}_{3} \\\\  & =3+\\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\  & =4+\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083277 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049404}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4541aede-24e3-55bf-b6d5-c3d9310def60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232180}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232180} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e514d3-a948-599d-bfa7-67a5488c061b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2d13daa6-87fd-5dec-89b3-0bd50ec97fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-133330" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{f(6n\\pi +\\pi )}{(\\sqrt{3})^{n}}$ is an infinite geometric series, compute its sum.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we evaluated the first few terms of the series:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=\\sqrt{3},\\,{a}_{3}=1\n$$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=3,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1-\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217152 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050389}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b48c2e3-9251-5785-b041-4c97e6794aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232182} :answer-field/id #uuid "281f8536-33dc-51c5-a7e3-f110ba83f1c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232181} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb2211fb-c929-53cd-928d-85805476a641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232184} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceab62f6-5b1c-5b06-96b1-be2b13062d59" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f091b74-ef3a-5709-9d1d-1134b992f7be" :question/math-academy-id "q-142979" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f065f31c629cbd21a6beffe013c8786a4e8165f6dff09bde72cd3e61c8ae8a62.png)\n\nThe function $f$ is part of the exponential curve $y={e}^{x-a}+b$ shown in the figure above. Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the horizontal asymptote of $y={e}^{x-a}+b$ is $y=b.$ \n\nIn the figure above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=3.$ Hence, $b=3.$\n\nNext, we use the point $(5,4)$ to determine the value of $a.$\nSubstituting $(5,4)$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5-a}+3 & =4 \\\\ {e}^{5-a} & =1 \\\\ 5-a & =0 \\\\ a & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=5$ and $b=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119767 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048329}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ffbf44d-57b7-546f-94f8-85edf05246ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232186} :answer-field/id #uuid "33cf8a89-f8a7-5585-ac44-c4b719df7a1d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9cda6266-e443-504c-b9bb-5d0d73939694" :question/math-academy-id "q-142980" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cced23601cd4ccb10839c503dc8068c7edf9a4f9daa2f83d2263c37abd7fe86d.png)\n\nThe function $g$ is part of the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{sec}x+3.$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{13\\pi }{6}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13\\pi }{6}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{13\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $y=\\operatorname{sec}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+3=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}+3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054797 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2704639d-8404-5236-8832-304e6f32f617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232188} :answer-field/id #uuid "22dcac90-e8f7-5473-b330-065b7b172050" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "38b427b2-8dd6-5cd1-9c34-7c699a8eaa03" :question/math-academy-id "q-142991" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1ada2941e9819e75218f9995d1913d535889c71b29efbf39ef73d91f9113205.png)\n\nThe function $h$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$5,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $h$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n$x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have to solve $h(x)=5,$ or equivalently, $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3=5.$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}$ by subtracting $3$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3 & =5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $\\frac{3}{2}$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x})^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{9}+3 & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3+3 & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 2+3 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=9$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3=5$  is $x=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078486 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048176}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fac4dff-e308-5000-a58c-08c5698f21a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232190}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232190} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a40b009-fce8-5035-b4fb-515fdd5c8944" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232189} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e9fa6e5-7df2-502f-9064-d3f0a0078d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232192} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a1cebcc-6735-5b18-be7c-9534d51958b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232191} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5ab6d9-d299-5ca8-b5f6-12eac24bf208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232194} :answer-field/id #uuid "28bd0e9c-1353-5f11-aa84-46c7662152b8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232193} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79f74227-688b-5b74-a866-92a91e0714aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232196} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee485090-861b-5e92-a4ae-f538bd688642" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232195}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8e52bb74-96e5-5af8-8c51-5d13739bdb51" :question/math-academy-id "q-142992" :question/problem "What is the largest union of intervals on which the function describing the stock price is continuous?\n\n$x\\in [\\,\\,0\\,\\,,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\cup $\n$($\n{{field-2}}$\\,,$\n{{field-3}}$)\\cup $\n$($\n{{field-4}}$\\,,\\,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(0,5.4)$, $(5.4,7.2)$ and $(7.2,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=0.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $[0,5.4).$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on $x\\in [0,5.4)\\cup (5.4,7.2)\\cup (7.2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059862 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "326746f8-b33a-5b7e-b97c-e73b697c6c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232198} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e0d94b-00e1-582c-a13d-ce25783110ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9342c495-5eb7-5bb2-becb-1daa8a37b8ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-142993" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $h(x).$\n\n${h}^{'}(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We apply the sum, constant multiple, and power rules as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{(\\frac{1}{2}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064154 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048835}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b018299-7007-54eb-bc89-99c9efa7a6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1/9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232200}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232200} :answer-field/id #uuid "60e7eb70-1c1f-5c79-88b8-72f4909c2ea9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5d565b38-f76f-5424-8185-fe15e9499b1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142994" :question/problem "What was the exact rate of change of the stock's value at week $9?$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ thousand dollars per week." :question/worked-solution "The function ${h}^{'}(x)$ models the rate of change of the value of the stock, applicable in the domain of $h(x).$\n\nThus, the rate of change of the value of the stock at $x=9$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(9) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the stock price was $\\frac{1}{9}$ thousand dollars per week."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129195 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048435}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c280511a-69d2-581b-bfee-49029a89a69c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232202} :answer-field/id #uuid "11d38ec2-9eea-5ada-a086-2314647e71a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b30e7a6-f1f7-5704-b2de-85aad447731d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143040" :question/problem "The broker predicts that, in the future, the stock will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $k(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=k(x):$\n\n![](images/9b/9b81a63c0681b10343cf600cdfc17636c797aeb9b15920da4368d26643827ab5.png)\n\nThe graph shows that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }k(x)=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064168 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049481}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "716b9760-7403-5664-a541-6f49fb28dac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232204} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e017c6d-4e9c-5cb6-a364-f311476d6242" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a718be7-eb73-54f8-925e-728120665d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-143041" :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ equals{{field-1}} $+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[3]{x}={x}^{1/3}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4/3})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4/3-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064283 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903bc72c-9bed-5507-9ca2-4b5a360e273e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{csc}^{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232206} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bb51450-3228-5dc2-96d0-2e4189e141cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f83dffe-8754-54db-b2a6-b0e1c17bfbe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-143516" :question/problem "The acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n$a(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}t$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nv(t)=4{csc}^{2}(t).\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $v(t)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nv=4{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}=8u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =8u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t) \\\\  & =(8\\operatorname{csc}t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}t\\operatorname{cot}t) \\\\  & =-8{csc}^{2}t\\operatorname{cot}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233487 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096845 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec192c70-e569-50ff-8384-af86c7a4c091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2t\\operatorname{sin}(3t)+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232208} :answer-field/id #uuid "591ef578-25d3-5e0e-9800-62864943c1c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "86ba5562-5090-57e8-ba8d-4732a237a9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143518" :question/problem "Find the acceleration function ${a}_{2}(t)$ of the second particle.\n\n${a}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "In part 3, we saw that the derivative of a particle's velocity with respect to time gives its acceleration.\n\nTo differentiate ${v}_{2}(t),$ we require the product rule. Let $g(t)={t}^{2}$ and $h(t)=\\operatorname{sin}(3t).$ Then\n\n$$\n{g}^{'}(t)=2t,\\,{h}^{'}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}(3t).\n$$\n\nWe used the chain rule to calculate ${h}^{'}(t).$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}(t) & ={v}_{2}^{'}(t) \\\\  & ={g}^{'}(t)\\cdot h(t)+g(t)\\cdot {h}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(2t)(\\operatorname{sin}(3t))+({t}^{2})(3\\operatorname{cos}(3t)) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{sin}(3t)+3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064181 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048777}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fab2b00-89b9-5e37-acd4-cacd09480aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cot}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232210}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232210} :answer-field/id #uuid "741876d0-ec1d-5a78-b6aa-658f559e821f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7c3f30b1-6acc-552c-b509-78b7cd37e409" :question/math-academy-id "q-143522" :question/problem "$\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\,\\,=\\,${{field-1}} $+\\text{ }C$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int {csc}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cot}t+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 4{csc}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t & =4\\int {csc}^{2}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{cot}t)+C \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cot}t+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184205 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050848}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ad811d-ca27-5d62-b4a5-d775b5eb346d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232212}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232212} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea8b4f40-f65d-53dd-ab9a-e1a2c3f018e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e321859-6eb2-57d7-beeb-a4c0fe12ce67" :question/math-academy-id "q-143531" :question/problem "Find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n4{csc}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=4{(2)}^{2}=16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119767 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048329}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6deff802-2dde-5593-8b8b-718b74b66f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232214}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232214} :answer-field/id #uuid "731e7fab-e8a4-573b-b8d1-b20ee06b40ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e8db7f91-3f9d-5ea8-be71-4cd6aec7f241" :question/math-academy-id "q-143534" :question/problem "Another particle has a velocity function given by ${v}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(3t).$ Evaluate the velocity of this particle at $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n${v}_{2}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}$" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the function ${v}_{2}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$, we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)={\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{5\\pi }{2}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)={\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115186 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049442}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94f805b0-4ee5-587e-8377-b061d2cabe9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232216} :answer-field/id #uuid "c11291ac-5fb7-5eee-a4de-2b88979f53b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79eabba8-29ab-5349-b14e-c6f84374b2df" :question/math-academy-id "q-143539" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{t\\to \\pi /6}{v}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\pi /6}{v}_{2}(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to \\pi /6}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}(3t) \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124670 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050916}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5fc5178-00c0-5669-99bc-341b9a8d7861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232218} :answer-field/id #uuid "95fd8822-2a7f-5ae1-be5e-16dd2262237e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7a27b627-9fae-5448-872d-db537d0f8b45" :question/math-academy-id "q-143540" :question/problem "${lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/retired true :question/worked-solution "Substituting $t=\\pi $ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}v(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}4{csc}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =4{csc}^{2}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =4\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}t.$\n\n![](images/36/361e8bf3b622fc2e73be712dae4ec5d189bd15eecbd33c9295db7365bcf71943.png)\n\nAs $t$ approaches $\\pi $ from the left, the values of $y=\\operatorname{csc}t$ are positive and grow without bound. \n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}t=\\infty \n$$\n\nApplying the product and power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}4{csc}^{2}(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}4\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}{csc}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}(t))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying by the positive constant $4$ does not change the sign of the limit, so we have\n\n$$\n4\\cdot {({lim}_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}\\operatorname{csc}(t))}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049637}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce7ddb80-9f57-58b8-b6b2-340adc0e1522" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232220} :answer-field/id #uuid "abadb571-40ba-5f47-b836-04939a4bcdda" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f802c6a-0ecb-5308-98ae-ade4a27f0f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-166172" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term of the polynomial and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(2x-{x}^{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-(\\infty ) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604145 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604000 :multistep/context "Consider the quadratic function $f(x)=2x-{x}^{2}.$"}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078536 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050225}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1b91d76-d966-549b-86c8-fc23512b102e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232222} :answer-field/id #uuid "bba7fb16-fce4-5117-a3a9-c8ab0e442179" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2cd3ecc6-8656-5f05-af32-2a74c173c48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166173" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)}.$  If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit directly by substituting $x=2,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}}{2(2)-(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since there is a radical in the numerator, we can evaluate the limit by first multiplying the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{2x-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})}{(2x-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{x-2}{x(2-x)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{-(2-x)}{x(2-x)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{2-x}}{x\\cancel{(2-x)}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604101 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604000 :multistep/context "Consider the quadratic function $f(x)=2x-{x}^{2}.$"}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064337 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049362}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "641f9fde-09aa-58b3-89f9-ae161a70dfea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232224} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d822978-5d73-57d5-ba9f-f827cc8219e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "be45abcf-bf92-543a-b55e-1914daedd097" :question/math-academy-id "q-166174" :question/problem "The instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n${f}^{'}(3)={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}={lim}_{x\\to 3}(-x-$\n{{field-1}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the expression under the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(3) & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{[2x-{x}^{2}]-[2(3)-(3)^{2}]}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{-{x}^{2}+2x+3}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{-({x}^{2}-2x-3)}{x-3}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\left(\\frac{-(x+1)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{\\cancel{x-3}}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}(-x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604119 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604000 :multistep/context "Consider the quadratic function $f(x)=2x-{x}^{2}.$"}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124468 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050653}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d433e385-b168-544e-b152-8744d0818317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232226} :answer-field/id #uuid "132e25bb-c8f7-5cb8-a5d0-4d71cb9b5e9d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "143aa4ab-13c2-5621-93fc-49a760ac6e05" :question/math-academy-id "q-166175" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 3}(-x-1)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}(-x-1) & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}x-{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}1 \\\\  & =-(3)-1 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604127 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604000 :multistep/context "Consider the quadratic function $f(x)=2x-{x}^{2}.$"}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087881 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048904}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7e5829-7a5b-54f0-a454-f692198adfc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232228} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b1c941-3cdf-5f20-a5e2-91ce04d6d080" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "15e574b1-222b-5544-8b25-29a00c2984c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-166176" :question/problem "The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\,2\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,h\\,).$" :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the expression under the limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-(x+h)^{2}-(2x-{x}^{2})}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2x+2h-{x}^{2}-2xh-{h}^{2}-2x+{x}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2h-2hx-{h}^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{h(2-2x-h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(2-2x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604182 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604000 :multistep/context "Consider the quadratic function $f(x)=2x-{x}^{2}.$"}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124468 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050653}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c60332d-f74e-56bb-b115-b48d0680cdab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232230}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232230} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b6b1393-9441-5b28-8139-628a148ab39d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3af21d2f-3d78-5cd4-970d-81d6f9303963" :question/math-academy-id "q-166177" :question/problem "${f}^{'}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(2-2x-h)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first apply the sum rule, and then apply the constant rule and compute the limits of the power functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}(2-2x-h) & ={lim}_{h\\to \\,0}(2-2x)-{lim}_{h\\to \\,0}h \\\\  & =2-2x-0 \\\\  & =2-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604144 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604000 :multistep/context "Consider the quadratic function $f(x)=2x-{x}^{2}.$"}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049386}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc427d78-c312-5005-9bff-1c36819c6a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232232} :answer-field/id #uuid "82bac253-ff77-5a3d-b8ab-a300114e1ab9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6744c619-d60e-5bea-8836-86206d5de51b" :question/math-academy-id "q-166178" :question/problem "![](images/17/17c57ba55e06207350ab93c8cac79bf22c48ba94e73ce4f914cd809b74b7d862.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above (not drawn to scale). Estimate the area $A$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n$A\\,\\approx \\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/41/41fde96921443be6c1efc28800e921a189fbe32d8a43667f6a85e7cf399eb745.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\approx (\\overbrace{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{width}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $0.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & \\approx (0.75+1+0.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604133 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604000 :multistep/context "Consider the quadratic function $f(x)=2x-{x}^{2}.$"}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096629 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048988}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2382214a-8edf-5e59-b63e-de93325691d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232234} :answer-field/id #uuid "0df16cb5-5008-5b8a-9f9d-0959431fc715" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "292bd1f6-009d-5af5-9e90-4207fa217d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-166179" :question/problem "Write the expression for the Riemann sum from part 7 in sigma notation.\n\nThe right Riemann sum $\\,=\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(i-$\n{{field-1}} ${i}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nIn our case, we have $n=4$ subintervals, $a=0,$ $b=2,$ and $Δx=0.5.$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}f(0+i\\cdot 0.5)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5f(0.5i) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(2(0.5i)-{(0.5i)}^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}0.5(i-0.25{i}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604095 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604000 :multistep/context "Consider the quadratic function $f(x)=2x-{x}^{2}.$"}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096630 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048862}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ae62458-144d-58ac-b5ef-69e0641e905f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232236} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1720898-832c-5cfb-913a-4c57e1578c09" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232235} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55fc9aad-1c59-578c-9531-f1d1199584d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232238}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232238} :answer-field/id #uuid "17609a4d-12e2-5eb2-bd09-ba290e16cea2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232237} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6158d52-5bbf-5ec8-8c14-9ae62085c1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232240} :answer-field/id #uuid "549a644c-b849-5c6d-a0ee-62851f51a70e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59889aa8-e689-5c28-96d5-47737d31b9b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-166180" :question/problem "The exact value of the area $A$ from part 7 can be expressed using definite integral notation as\n\n$A={\\int }_{a}^{b}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{d}x,\\,\\,$ where  \n$a\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "We can express the area $A$ as the definite integral of \n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nbetween \n\n* the lower limit $x=0,$ and \n\n* the upper limit $x=2.$ \n\nThis gives\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :step/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604172 :multistep/_steps {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604000 :multistep/context "Consider the quadratic function $f(x)=2x-{x}^{2}.$"}}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078532 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050396}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55e89a39-907f-5cdf-9a4c-ae82a376bcce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100a+10b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232242} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c4b0132-a705-55b3-a51e-b548ae2b7d0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f0a4647-d3c8-50cb-98c3-9b2b4cd8c029" :question/math-academy-id "q-167192" :question/problem "In terms of $a$ and $b,\\,p(10)=\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=10$ into the expression for $p(x)$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =a{x}^{2}+bx \\\\ p(10) & =a(10)^{2}+b(10) \\\\  & =100a+10b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(10)=100a+10b.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064348 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf5bd8b4-0bdb-5b6f-b9ee-a77a23240073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "480-10a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232244}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232244} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f2aa387-5ca3-5658-8667-37976530df6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a10ab92-ad60-50b9-b3dd-ee735a188387" :question/math-academy-id "q-167193" :question/problem "If the company sells ten couches in a week, the weekly profit will be $$4\\,800.$ Find an expression for $b$ in terms of $a.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that $p(10)=100a+10b.$ So, we need to solve the equation $100a+10b=4\\,800$ for the parameter $b.$\n\nWe need to isolate the variable $b.$ To do this, we treat $a$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $100a$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100a+10b & =4\\,800 \\\\ 100a+10b-100a & =4\\,800-100a \\\\ 10b & =4\\,800-100a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10b & =4\\,800-100a \\\\ \\frac{10b}{10} & =\\frac{4\\,800-100a}{10} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{10}b}{\\cancel{10}} & =\\frac{4\\,800}{10}-\\frac{100a}{10} \\\\ b & =480-10a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $b=480-10a.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d447476e-6786-52c0-9a5b-aa6aa6b42f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232246}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232246} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6f81397-282c-55bf-a574-31b7a7eb6872" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232245} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d9ee9f-e333-5485-a7ba-ff333f3a6931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232248} :answer-field/id #uuid "e81a89a6-5e45-5c38-944f-815f8eebc873" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ba03c851-f080-5b98-bcba-fc2174ffc663" :question/math-academy-id "q-167194" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $20b+400a=7\\,200.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=480-10a \\\\ 20b+400a &=7\\,200\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=480-10a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20b+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 20(480-10a)+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600-200a+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600+200a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 200a & =-2\\,400 \\\\ a & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-12$ into $b=480-10a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =480-10(-12) \\\\ b & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-12,\\,b=600.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068701 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "466c8e2d-153c-55f7-a977-4787d9b2772e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232250}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232250} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f3cb2c2-2995-5313-8a78-2f1d796ba8ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2d2408b1-1be6-5416-967f-0d4cd2520114" :question/math-academy-id "q-167195" :question/problem "Determine the weekly profit if the company produces $40$ couches in a week.\n\nThe company will earn$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$dollars." :question/worked-solution "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-12{x}^{2}+600x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=40$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(40) & =-12(40)^{2}+600(40) \\\\  & =-19\\,200+24\\,000 \\\\  & =4\\,800\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$4\\,800.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064323 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23032e7c-326e-5437-9937-76f3a526071d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-50x+600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232252} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b055d5b-a5f6-5237-8160-759c230d4419" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a3801e84-afaa-5faf-b271-995c9174951f" :question/math-academy-id "q-167196" :question/problem "The manager wants to determine the number of couches the company should produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$7\\,200.$ The required number of sofas $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n12{x}^{2}-600x+7\\,200=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be factored as $\\,$$12\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We factor $12{x}^{2}-600x+7\\,200$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(12{x}^{2},600x,7\\,200)=12.\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-600x+7\\,200 &= 12\\cdot ({x}^{2})+12\\cdot (-50x)+12\\cdot (600)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot ({x}^{2})+12\\cdot (-50x)+12\\cdot (600) &= 12({x}^{2}-50x+600)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-50x+600.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054658 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049074}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a994178-6db5-55ce-9a26-f64f8d0cc516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232254} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6820608-aa59-56cb-8642-86232282c3bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0ffec26b-e532-5d96-b5b9-1b0ec13b40ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-167197" :question/problem "Find the minimum number of couches the company needs to produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$7\\,200.$\n\nThe company needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ couches per week." :question/worked-solution "From part 5, the required number of sofas $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n12\\cdot ({x}^{2}-50x+600)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $12,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-50x+600=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(600)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-50).$ The required factors are $(-20)$ and $(-30).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-50x=-20x-30x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-50x+600 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-20x-30x+600 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x-30+600 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-20x)+(-30x+600) & =0 \\\\ x(x-20)-30(x-20) & =0 \\\\ (x-30)(x-20) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=20$ or $x=30.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $20$ couches per week to make a profit of $$7\\,200.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133748 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049399}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c15517f-3c08-5945-bb23-0fa1f7055757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3n+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232256}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232256} :answer-field/id #uuid "746ca86c-4b97-5add-b306-967f06262ba6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b790a880-e2fa-57c8-a6dd-5f53a78ff028" :question/math-academy-id "q-167198" :question/problem "The company wants to increase its output by implementing a new production model. The number of couches ${a}_{n}$ produced in the $n$th week since the implementation of the new model is given by the arithmetic sequence\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+3,\\,{a}_{1}=11,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nWhat is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence?\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=11,$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+3 \\\\  & =11+3 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =14-11 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute ${a}_{1}=11$ and $d=3$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =11+(n-1)(3) \\\\  & =11+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3n+8,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078532 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050396}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03cea2a5-2b91-5e00-ac07-5c1c01145ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "400a+20b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232258}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232258} :answer-field/id #uuid "00da10fc-87c8-5645-8e34-e6298214b5d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b3c7128d-0cfb-56e5-8453-19776c329fec" :question/math-academy-id "q-167208" :question/problem "In terms of $a$ and $b,\\,p(20)=\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=20$ into the expression for $p(x)$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =a{x}^{2}+bx \\\\ p(20) & =a(20)^{2}+b(20) \\\\  & =400a+20b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(20)=400a+20b.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064348 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3776145f-02c9-5a7b-8885-f23017d70e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "240-20a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232260}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232260} :answer-field/id #uuid "a60deb7e-661b-5b84-9b31-5b322ff6eaab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "099c25b9-f15b-5caa-9333-8704de328b9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167209" :question/problem "If the bakery sells twenty cakes in a week, the weekly profit will be $$4\\,800.$ Find an expression for $b$ in terms of $a.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that $p(20)=400a+20b.$ So, we need to solve the equation $400a+20b=4\\,800$ for the parameter $b.$\n\nWe need to isolate the variable $b.$ To do this, we treat $a$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $400a$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}400a+20b & =4\\,800 \\\\ 400a+20b-400a & =4\\,800-400a \\\\ 20b & =4\\,800-400a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $20:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20b & =4\\,800-400a \\\\ \\frac{20b}{20} & =\\frac{4\\,800-400a}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{20}b}{\\cancel{20}} & =\\frac{4\\,800}{20}-\\frac{400a}{20} \\\\ b & =240-20a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $b=240-20a.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f6ebe2-11a4-50a4-afb6-e54166d6b757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232262} :answer-field/id #uuid "d89c06a2-d40d-59be-aba2-b51cfcb77eb2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232261} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d5e96b-1699-54bb-87d3-abe3b34a48dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232264} :answer-field/id #uuid "80788b1d-1ccd-5c42-ae32-41ab3f383746" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d104504e-e7b5-56df-8154-499bbc1d3f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-167210" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $30b+900a=6\\,000.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=240-20a \\\\ 30b+900a &=6\\,000\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=240-20a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30b+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 30(240-20a)+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200-600a+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200+300a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 300a & =-1\\,200 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-4$ into $b=240-20a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =240-20(-4) \\\\ b & =320\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=320.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068701 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95053f63-92b5-5603-9654-5fabb23af5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232266} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc3836e-795a-574d-8a02-0f1ec28238b8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "38c00f37-7b86-52f4-a8e6-fd7ce270a1e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-167211" :question/problem "Determine the weekly profit if the bakery produces $35$ cakes in a week.\n\nThe company will earn$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$dollars." :question/worked-solution "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-4{x}^{2}+320x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=35$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(35) & =-4(35)^{2}+320(35) \\\\  & =-4\\,900+11\\,200 \\\\  & =6\\,300\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$6\\,300.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064323 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69d6f845-951b-5e88-86b4-5c6cb72f54c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-80x+1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232268} :answer-field/id #uuid "867ecee0-07f2-54a4-b55f-fb546d4b264d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "91eb1d3c-008d-5a1b-b2ee-5b1886437bbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-167212" :question/problem "The manager wants to determine the number of cakes the bakery should produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$6\\,000.$ The required number of cakes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be factored as $\\,$$4\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We factor $4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(4{x}^{2},320x,6\\,000)=4\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-320x+6\\,000 &= 4\\cdot ({x}^{2})+4\\cdot (-80x)+4\\cdot (1\\,500)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({x}^{2})+4\\cdot (-80x)+4\\cdot (1\\,500) &= 4({x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054658 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049074}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04180b05-225f-558e-97e1-8af3ff13b1b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232270} :answer-field/id #uuid "524a2388-f0b7-56b0-999f-38513dc1e3dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a3dea998-c15c-559b-b546-7507c1a654f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167213" :question/problem "Find the minimum number of cakes the bakery needs to produce per week to make a weekly profit of $$6\\,000.$\n\nThe bakery needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ cakes per week." :question/worked-solution "From part 5, the required number of cakes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n4\\cdot ({x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $4,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(1\\,500)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-80).$ The required factors are $(-30)$ and $(-50).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-80x=-30x-50x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-80x+1\\,500 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-30x-50x+1\\,500 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-30x-50x+1\\,500 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-30x)+(-50x+1\\,500) & =0 \\\\ x(x-30)-50(x-30) & =0 \\\\ (x-30)(x-50)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=30$ or $x=50.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the bakery needs to produce $30$ cakes per week to make a profit of $$6\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133748 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049399}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d709b14b-caf5-5c8b-8e20-3a10ed658784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4n+15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232272} :answer-field/id #uuid "55bf61b4-4230-5565-b65f-f49c99e18935" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c0bc5995-60c4-58c6-82c2-947a9258f754" :question/math-academy-id "q-167214" :question/problem "The bakery wants to increase its output by implementing a new production model. The number of cakes ${a}_{n}$ produced in the $n$th week since the implementation of the new model is given by the arithmetic sequence\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+4,\\,{a}_{1}=19,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nWhat is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence?\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=19,$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+4 \\\\  & =19+4 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =23-19 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute ${a}_{1}=19$ and $d=4$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =19+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =19+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=4n+15,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078532 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050396}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7583ed5-9f1c-5ba3-a605-a92198c53c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25a+5b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232274} :answer-field/id #uuid "398c7956-5b09-5d93-932a-f8b6e8232de2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "78341531-2ea3-5ea7-aec7-ad8c60ab7bd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-167224" :question/problem "In terms of $a$ and $b,\\,p(5)=\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=5$ into the expression for $p(x)$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =a{x}^{2}+bx \\\\ p(5) & =a(5)^{2}+b(5) \\\\  & =25a+5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(5)=25a+5b.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064348 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7623977-23e1-5c02-873e-8ded4f7e9446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "75-5a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232276} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f2dc8f-d307-5512-ae0b-ca8f1cf64201" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ecc28f9-c0cb-551c-a99f-4673aa56d011" :question/math-academy-id "q-167225" :question/problem "If the company sells five luxury white chocolate boxes a day, the daily profit will be $$375.$ Find an expression for $b$ in terms of $a.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that $p(5)=25a+5b.$ So, we need to solve the equation $25a+5b=375$ for the parameter $b.$\n\nWe need to isolate the variable $b.$ To do this, we treat $a$ as though it were a constant and apply the addition and multiplication principles as usual.\n\nFirst, we subtract $25a$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25a+5b & =375 \\\\ 25a+5b-25a & =375-25a \\\\ 5b & =375-25a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5b & =375-25a \\\\ \\frac{5b}{5} & =\\frac{375-25a}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}b}{\\cancel{5}} & =\\frac{375}{5}-\\frac{25a}{5} \\\\ b & =75-5a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $b=75-5a.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b8ef99-22cc-5e49-9884-ae62824c5059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232278} :answer-field/id #uuid "194751b6-0ff6-5047-a6e8-f7e3cfbe1451" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232277} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39731897-add5-5068-a83a-649fb0aa6e48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232280}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232280} :answer-field/id #uuid "03a21b91-803e-51b3-8b0d-859c1f623d17" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6afc1799-60d6-5f69-94a5-994731079a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-167226" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $70a+9b=600.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=75-5a \\\\ 70a+9b &=600\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=75-5a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70a+9b & =600 \\\\ 70a+9(75-5a) & =600 \\\\ 70a+675-45a & =600 \\\\ 25a+675 & =600 \\\\ 25a & =-75 \\\\ a & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-3$ into $b=75-5a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =75-5(-3) \\\\ b & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-3,\\,b=90.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068701 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2b6adfa-0a1d-579b-9f6f-0bc1d216329a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232282} :answer-field/id #uuid "310c74a5-831e-5cfd-8546-eb6ab77cba56" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7089dfb8-efcb-5e49-b43c-3457d2e3dfff" :question/math-academy-id "q-167227" :question/problem "Determine the daily profit if the company produces $25$ luxury white chocolate boxes in a day.\n\nThe company will earn$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$dollars." :question/worked-solution "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-3{x}^{2}+90x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=25$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(25) & =-3(25)^{2}+90(25) \\\\  & =-1\\,875+2\\,250 \\\\  & =375\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$375.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064323 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d120728-cd1a-5fb3-837e-5e6b7b70b38a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-30x+200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232284}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232284} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b0b7f3-bcd5-5ef0-9241-064c93983260" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "02bf8797-4e8d-58c0-a3b6-dde491cb5f0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167228" :question/problem "The manager wants to determine the number of luxury white chocolate boxes the company should produce per day to make a daily profit of $$600.$ The required number of white chocolate boxes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-90x+600=0.\n$$\n\nThis equation can be factored as $\\,$$3\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We factor $3{x}^{2}-90x+600$ using the greatest common factor (GCF).\n\nComputing the GCF, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(3{x}^{2},90x,600)=3\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-90x+600 &= 3\\cdot ({x}^{2})+3\\cdot (-30x)+3\\cdot (200)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot ({x}^{2})+3\\cdot (-30x)+3\\cdot (200) &= 3({x}^{2}-30x+200)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{2}-30x+200.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054658 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049074}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9622962-272d-5db3-8a3a-cb49b0788a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232286} :answer-field/id #uuid "10627fba-0198-5f1f-9ba5-1aa7386550a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f4b49fe3-dd04-578b-b82f-ceeb67c1d838" :question/math-academy-id "q-167229" :question/problem "Find the minimum number of luxury white chocolate boxes the company needs to produce per day to make a daily profit of $$600.$\n\nThe company needs to produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ luxury white chocolate boxes per day." :question/worked-solution "From part 5, the required number of white chocolate boxes $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n3\\cdot ({x}^{2}-30x+200)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $3,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-30x+200=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(200)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-30).$ The required factors are $(-10)$ and $(-20).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-30x=-10x-20x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-30x+200 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x-20x+200 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x-20x+200 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-10x)+(-20x+200) & =0 \\\\ x(x-10)-20(x-10) & =0 \\\\ (x-10)(x-20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=10$ or $x=20.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $10$ luxury white chocolate boxes per day to make a profit of $$600.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133748 :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049399}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9215e025-398f-5e3f-8251-afeae67ee5cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5n+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232288} :answer-field/id #uuid "c28daac8-a780-5064-aa63-b63c53fc1c43" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a98502d3-3e98-5775-8e8a-b8371afdeb70" :question/math-academy-id "q-167230" :question/problem "The company wants to increase its output by implementing a new production model. The number of luxury white chocolate boxes ${a}_{n}$ produced on the $n$th day since the implementation of the new model is given by the arithmetic sequence\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+5,\\,{a}_{1}=10,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nWhat is the explicit formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence?\n\n${a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The explicit formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\\,n\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nso we need to determine the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nWe are given that the first term is ${a}_{1}=10,$ and we can use the recursive rule to compute the second term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+5 \\\\  & =10+5 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the first and second terms, we can compute the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =15-10 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute ${a}_{1}=10$ and $d=5$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =10+(n-1)(5) \\\\  & =10+5n-5 \\\\  & =5n+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5n+5,\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$"}]}
